Scope, stated as a default rather than as a list. EVERY .md file in this
collection is under this licence, EXCEPT these, which are MIT and are named in
LICENSE:

    templates/    examples/    build.py    .claude-plugin/    LICENSE    LICENSE-DOCS

This was an enumerated list until 2026-09-10 and the list had gone stale: seven
prose files added after it was written were absent from it, including both of
the mutually exclusive layer rule sets, which by LICENSE's own fallback made
them MIT and removed the attribution requirement from them without anyone
deciding that. A list of covered files has to be edited every time a file is
added and silently under-covers when it is not. A default does not. The build
now checks that every shipped file is covered by exactly one of the two
licences, so this cannot drift again.

You are free to share and adapt these, including commercially, provided you
give appropriate credit, link to this licence, and indicate if changes were
made. Attribution should name the collection and the version, because a claim
corrected between versions is otherwise indistinguishable from one that still
holds. CITATION.cff carries the citation metadata.

Why the split. The MIT licence is written for software and permits use without
attribution, which is the wrong default for a document collection whose entire
argument is that claims should be traceable to who made them and when. CC BY
requires the credit that makes a corrected claim followable back to its source.
The templates, examples and build script stay MIT because they are meant to be
copied into other repositories without a licence obligation travelling with
them.

LOCAL.md, where present, is under neither licence. It names a specific personal
toolchain, machine paths and account access, is excluded from every
distribution, and is not part of the shareable artefact.
